---
label: Implementation overview
---

Server-rendered lists become sortable through the `sortable-lists` Stimulus
controller suite. A drag or a move menu action sends one request describing
where the row landed; the server persists the order and streams the list
back. The same suite drives a single admin list, the Backlogs board and the
nested Project Attributes settings.

This page documents the markup and HTTP contract for wiring the controllers
by hand. The **Design guidance** tab covers when drag and drop is appropriate
and what users expect from it.

## Before you wire anything

**Prefer the component API where it exists.** `BorderBoxListComponent` is
gaining `sortable_list:` / `sortable_item:` options that emit this contract
for you ([DREAM-805](https://community.openproject.org/wp/DREAM-805)). Once
those land they cover a single list and sibling lists of rows the server can
reorder. Wire by hand only for what they do not reach: several item types
under one root, per-type move endpoints, non-`BorderBoxList` markup such as
table rows or cards, or optimistic placement.

**Do not use `generic-drag-and-drop`.** The Dragula-based controller is
deprecated and closed to new consumers. The API reference tab has a migration
table for its remaining call sites.

## Anatomy

Four controllers divide the work and find each other through Stimulus
outlets, never by walking the DOM:

| Controller | Placed on | Owns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `sortable-lists` | one wrapper per page region | the move URL, the request, rollback on failure, the outlet selectors for the three children |
| `sortable-lists--list` | each list | the list's type, its id, which item type it accepts, where a drop on empty space lands |
| `sortable-lists--item` | each row that can move | the row's id, type and accessible label; the drag handle and preview; the move menu actions |
| `sortable-lists--scrollable` | whatever element scrolls | auto-scrolling while a drag hovers its edge |

### A single list

<img src="<%= image_path("lookbook/sortable_lists_a.png") %>" alt="One root, one list, three rows; the root reaches the list and each row through outlets" width="460" style="max-width: 100%">

The minimum topology. Three controllers; the root reaches the other two
through outlets, never by traversing the DOM.

### Sibling lists in scrolling columns

<img src="<%= image_path("lookbook/sortable_lists_b.png") %>" alt="Two scroll columns under one root, each holding lists of the same type; the scrollable sits between root and lists" width="636" style="max-width: 100%">

Two scrolling columns, each holding several lists of one type. The
scrollables sit inside the root and are reached by outlet like every other
child; the root still addresses every item directly, however deep it sits.

### How the root reaches its children

<img src="<%= image_path("lookbook/sortable_lists_c.png") %>" alt="The root's three outlet selectors resolving to the list, item and scrollable controllers" width="760" style="max-width: 100%">

Nesting is DOM structure; this is how the controllers address each other.
The root declares one outlet per child type, so it holds a live collection
of each with no `closest()` and no `querySelector` walking. The selectors
are ordinary descendant selectors scoped to the root's own wrapper id, so
two roots on one page cannot capture each other's children:

```html
<div id="tasks"
     data-controller="sortable-lists"
     data-sortable-lists-move-url-template-value="<%%= move_url_template %>"
     data-sortable-lists-sortable-lists--list-outlet="#tasks [data-controller~='sortable-lists--list']"
     data-sortable-lists-sortable-lists--item-outlet="#tasks [data-controller~='sortable-lists--item']">
```

Because the selectors match at any depth, one root holds every list and
every item beneath it in one flat collection. A row's owning list is the
innermost list element that contains it. That single rule is what lets one
root serve nested lists as well as flat ones.

### Vocabulary

* **Sortable list, sortable item, sortable item type**: a list whose order the
  server persists, a row that takes part in that order, and the category a
  list accepts. A row is an item once it carries
  `data-sortable-lists--item-id-value`; that is what counts it and anchors
  its neighbors' drops. The `sortable-lists--item` controller on it is what
  handles dragging and its move menu. The `mobility` value determines
  whether it is `free`, `confined` to permitted destinations, or `fixed`
  in place. Fixed items still anchor their neighbors’ drops.
* **Drag source**: the original row while it is being dragged. **Drag
  preview**: the copy that follows the pointer.
* **Drop indicator**: the line marking where the row will land, drawn without
  reserving space. **Drop container**: the list-level outline shown while a
  compatible row hovers a list (`data-drop-container="active"`).
* **Reorder** happens within one list; a **move** changes list. In terms of
  the backend request they are identical.

## Choosing a scenario

* **One list**: rows reorder within a single list, such as admin enumerations
  or text transform actions.
* **Sibling lists**: several lists of the same kind, with rows moving between
  them, as in Backlogs.
* **Nested lists**: rows that are themselves lists, such as Project Attributes
  sections and their custom fields.
* **API reference**: every attribute, the backend request, the server half, the
  Angular counterpart, migration.
